                                jcr:primaryType="nt:unstructured"
                                jcr:title="Graduate CV Tips: How To Write A CV That Stands Out"
                                sling:resourceType="abbvie-com2/components/title/v2/title"
                                langAttribute="none"
                                type="h1"
                                variant="h"/>
                            <text_copy
                                cq:styleIds="[1659545825678]"
                                jcr:created="{Date}2023-03-03T16:34:59.807-05:00"
                                jcr:createdBy="chiucx"
                                jcr:lastModified="{Date}2026-07-22T12:06:27.846-04:00"
                                jcr:lastModifiedBy="vickekx"
                                jcr:primaryType="nt:unstructured"
                                sling:resourceType="abbvie-com2/components/text/v2/text"
                                langAttribute="none"
                                text="&lt;p>Writing your CV for a graduate role can feel daunting. You might be wondering:&lt;/p>&#xd;&#xa;&lt;p>&lt;i>What should I put on my CV if I don't have much experience? How long should a graduate CV be? What do employers actually look for? How can I make my CV stand out?&lt;/i>&lt;/p>&#xd;&#xa;&lt;p>The good news is that most employers aren't expecting years of experience. Instead, they're looking for evidence of your potential, your mindset and the skills you'll bring to the role.&lt;/p>&#xd;&#xa;"
                                textIsRich="true"/>
                        </container3>
                    </container2_copy>
